  "theory": {
"intro": "$\\sqrt{10}$ is the side length of a square of area $10$. Because $10$ lies between the perfect squares $9$ and $16$, $\\sqrt{10}$ lies between $3$ and $4$.",
"facts": [
"For nonnegative $a$ and $b$, If $a^{2}<x<b^{2}$, then $a<\\sqrt{x}<b$.\n\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\\large}]\n\\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}\n\\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-0.6,0)--(6.6,0);\n\\draw (1.5,0.12)--(1.5,-0.12);\n\\node[below] at (1.5,-0.12) {$a$};\n\\draw (4.5,0.12)--(4.5,-0.12);\n\\node[below] at (4.5,-0.12) {$b$};\n\\draw[very thick] (1.5,0)--(4.5,0);\n\\fill (2.7,0) circle (2.2pt);\n\\node[above] at (2.7,0.2) {$\\sqrt{x}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]",
"If $a^{3}<x<b^{3}$, then $a<\\sqrt[3]{x}<b$.",
"The landmarks are the perfect squares $1,4,9,16,25,36,49,64,81,100,\\ldots$ and the perfect cubes $1,8,27,64,125,216,343,\\ldots$",
"Perfect squares and cubes help you make a rough estimate. For example, $50$ is only a little above $49$, so $\\sqrt{50}$ is a little above $7$. For a nearest-whole-number answer, check the halfway value rather than comparing distances from the powered landmarks alone.",
"An estimate is not exact, so record a decimal value with $\\approx$, for example $\\sqrt{10}\\approx 3.16$."
],
"steps": [
"Find the two perfect squares (or cubes) the number lies between.",
"Take the root of each landmark to get the two whole numbers.",
"Refine the estimate using nearby squares or cubes. For the nearest whole number, square or cube the halfway value to check which side the root lies on. Check with a calculator when required."
],
"workedExamples": [
  {
    "question_text": "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does each root lie?\n(a) $\\sqrt{22}$\n(b) $\\sqrt[3]{90}$",
    "solution_text": "(a) $4^2=16<22<25=5^2$, so $4<\\sqrt{22}<5$.\n(b) $4^3=64<90<125=5^3$, so $4<\\sqrt[3]{90}<5$.\nUse perfect squares for a square root and perfect cubes for a cube root."
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Find each root exactly. Is an estimate needed?\n(a) $\\sqrt{81}$\n(b) $\\sqrt[3]{125}$",
    "solution_text": "(a) $81=9^2$, so $\\sqrt{81}=9$.\n(b) $125=5^3$, so $\\sqrt[3]{125}=5$.\nBoth numbers are exact landmarks, so no estimate is needed."
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Which whole number is $\\sqrt{56}$ closest to? Check your estimate.",
    "solution_text": "$49<56<64$, so $7<\\sqrt{56}<8$.\nThe halfway value between $7$ and $8$ is $7.5$.\n$7.5^2=56.25$ and $56<56.25$, so $\\sqrt{56}<7.5$.\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{56}$ is closer to $7$.\nA calculator check gives $\\sqrt{56}\\approx7.48$ to two decimal places.\nComparing distances from $49$ and $64$ alone would not settle which whole number is closest."
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Estimate each root between consecutive whole numbers, then check with a calculator and give the value to two decimal places. Plot their approximate positions on a number line.\n(a) $\\sqrt{8}$\n(b) $\\sqrt{38}$",
    "solution_text": "(a) $4<8<9$, so $2<\\sqrt{8}<3$.\nA calculator check gives $\\sqrt{8}\\approx2.83$.\n(b) $36<38<49$, so $6<\\sqrt{38}<7$.\nA calculator check gives $\\sqrt{38}\\approx6.16$.\nThe plotted positions are approximate.\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}\n\\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-0.4,0)--(6.64,0);\n\\foreach \\i in {0,...,8}{\\draw (0.78*\\i,0.12)--(0.78*\\i,-0.12);\\node[below] at (0.78*\\i,-0.12) {$\\i$};}\n\\fill (2.206,0) circle (2.2pt);\n\\node[above] at (2.206,0.2) {$\\sqrt{8}$};\n\\fill (4.808,0) circle (2.2pt);\n\\node[above] at (4.808,0.2) {$\\sqrt{38}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]"
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Show that $0.7<\\sqrt{0.6}<0.8$ without using a calculator.",
    "solution_text": "$0.7^2=0.49$ and $0.8^2=0.64$.\n$0.49<0.6<0.64$, so $\\sqrt{0.49}<\\sqrt{0.6}<\\sqrt{0.64}$.\nTherefore, $0.7<\\sqrt{0.6}<0.8$."
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Which is larger, $\\sqrt{70}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{200}$? Use bounds to explain.",
    "solution_text": "$64<70<81$, so $8<\\sqrt{70}<9$.\n$125<200<216$, so $5<\\sqrt[3]{200}<6$.\nThe intervals do not overlap: $\\sqrt{70}>8$ while $\\sqrt[3]{200}<6$.\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{70}$ is larger."
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Use strict bounds to answer each question.\n(a) $4<\\sqrt{n}<5$, where $n$ is a whole number. What is the largest possible $n$?\n(b) $2<\\sqrt[3]{m}<3$, where $m$ is a whole number. How many values can $m$ take?",
    "solution_text": "(a) Squaring the positive bounds gives $16<n<25$.\nThe largest whole number below $25$ is $24$.\n(b) Cubing the bounds gives $8<m<27$.\nThe whole numbers are $9$ to $26$, giving $26-9+1=18$ possible values.\nThe endpoints are excluded because the roots must lie strictly between the bounds."
  }
]
